        ⚡ Quick Answer: In-Browser PDF Compression
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-200 leading-relaxed">
        Compressing PDFs entirely inside the user's browser requires <strong>WebAssembly (WASM) ports of native C libraries</strong> like Ghostscript (via Emscripten) or pure TypeScript engines like <code>pdf-lib</code>. Ghostscript WASM achieves up to <strong>72% file size reduction</strong> by downsampling embedded raster images to 150 DPI and stripping redundant font tables, with 0 bytes ever leaving client memory.
      </p>
    </div>

